      "ballot_id": "B037",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Juan Maldacena", "Edward Witten"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "AdS/CFT is a remarkably powerful connection between quantum field theory and gravity that reshaped theoretical physics and enabled work on strongly coupled systems.",
        "attribution": "Maldacena gave the pivotal duality conjecture; Witten supplied foundational developments. The credited pair captures major contributions, though the intellectual history includes other important precursors.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been tested through extensive internal consistency and applications over decades, but its central quantum-gravity claims lack direct experimental confirmation.",
        "uncertainties": ["Whether a theoretical framework without direct empirical confirmation meets the prize's discovery standard."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Federico Capasso", "Jérôme Faist"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser was a distinct device invention that opened important mid-infrared and terahertz sources and became useful across spectroscopy and sensing.",
        "attribution": "Capasso led the concept and device development, with Faist central to its realization. Cho's foundational semiconductor heterostructure work is relevant background, though the specific invention has a strong claim to these two.",
        "maturity": "Its performance and applications have been demonstrated over many decades, so both the discovery and its lasting impact are secure.",
        "uncertainties": ["The boundary between the specific laser invention and earlier enabling heterostructure technology merits careful credit review."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Christopher Jarzynski", "Gavin E. Crooks", "Udo Seifert"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Fluctuation relations and stochastic thermodynamics extended thermodynamic reasoning to small, driven systems and gave the field a durable conceptual and quantitative foundation.",
        "attribution": "Jarzynski and Crooks established influential nonequilibrium relations, while Seifert developed a broad stochastic-thermodynamics framework. Evans's earlier fluctuation theorem is also an essential part of the history.",
        "maturity": "The theory has been tested in many experimental settings and has become a mature area of statistical physics.",
        "uncertainties": ["The discovery is a body of related results, so the exact scope and limits of a small laureate group need deliberation."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Francis Halzen"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "IceCube's discovery of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos opened a new observational channel on the high-energy universe and established neutrino astronomy as a field.",
        "attribution": "Halzen was the scientific leader and a central force behind the detector and program. The discovery depended on a large collaboration, making individual attribution a significant issue.",
        "maturity": "The signal has been confirmed and the field has developed through years of additional observations, though its source population is still being resolved.",
        "uncertainties": ["How to recognize the collaboration's essential work within the prize's limit on laureates."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B052",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Peter W. Shor", "Andrew M. Steane", "Alexei Kitaev"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed how quantum information can be protected against noise in principle and became indispensable to fault-tolerant quantum computing.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane independently introduced landmark quantum error-correcting codes, while Kitaev developed influential topological approaches to fault tolerance.",
        "maturity": "The central results are established, with increasingly capable experiments demonstrating error correction. Large-scale fault-tolerant computation remains a work in progress.",
        "uncertainties": ["The practical reach of fault-tolerant quantum computing is not yet settled, although the theoretical discovery itself is firm."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Michael V. Berry", "Yakir Aharonov"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase revealed that phase and gauge structure have observable consequences, with lasting influence across quantum and condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov's effect and Berry's general geometric phase are distinct contributions grouped under a broad theme; the credit for each should be assessed separately.",
        "maturity": "Both effects are well established experimentally and conceptually, with decades of use in many areas of physics.",
        "uncertainties": ["Combining two related but distinct discoveries in one nomination risks obscuring their separate histories and contributors."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Kam-Biu Luk", "Soo-Bong Kim", "Yifang Wang"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Measuring a nonzero reactor mixing angle completed a crucial part of the neutrino mixing picture and enabled important tests of neutrino oscillations.",
        "attribution": "The Daya Bay leadership and its collaboration deserve central recognition; credit must account for the parallel reactor experiments that also measured the parameter.",
        "maturity": "The result has been repeatedly confirmed and is now a standard part of the experimentally established neutrino framework.",
        "uncertainties": ["Several experiments contributed near the same time, complicating priority and individual attribution."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles L. Kane", "Eugene J. Mele", "Qi-Kun Xue"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Topological insulators established a significant new class of quantum matter, and the quantum anomalous Hall effect demonstrated a striking topological transport regime.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele made foundational theoretical contributions; Xue led decisive experimental realizations of the anomalous Hall effect. The combined scope spans related but distinct milestones.",
        "maturity": "Topological phases and the anomalous Hall effect are established experimentally, though their broader technological impact remains developing.",
        "uncertainties": ["The nomination combines topological-insulator theory with a specific experimental phase, so the prize scope and credit boundaries need scrutiny."]
      }
    }
  ]
